WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.300
Why does the R shape hold oxygen better than T?

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.700
The answer starts with a movement smaller than an atom.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.860
Hemoglobin is four chains, two α and two β.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.620
They pair up as two αβ dimers.

5
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:19.543
Each chain cradles one heme.

6
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:23.803
Heme is a ring, protoporphyrin IX, with an iron at its center.

7
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:27.553
The iron makes four bonds to the ring's nitrogens.

8
00:00:28.373 --> 00:00:31.993
A fifth bond goes to a histidine below, the proximal histidine.

9
00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:35.137
The sixth site is saved for oxygen.

10
00:00:36.257 --> 00:00:40.657
Across the pocket sits the distal histidine, which hydrogen-bonds to bound oxygen.

11
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.027
The iron must stay in its plus-two form.

12
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:49.087
Oxidized, it becomes methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen at all.

13
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.670
Step one. In T, the iron is high-spin and slightly too big for the ring's hole.

14
00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:00.490
It sits about 0.4 Å below the ring, toward the histidine.

15
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.733
That is about a seventh of the width of a water molecule.

16
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:10.707
When oxygen binds, the iron turns low-spin, shrinks, and rises into the plane.

17
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:17.687
Step two. The iron is bonded to the proximal histidine, so the histidine follows it.

18
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:22.917
Step three. That histidine sits on a helix, the F helix.

19
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:26.337
The helix shifts and presses on the neighboring subunit.

20
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:33.767
Step four. In Max Perutz's classic account, this is levered into a rotation of one

21
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.047
dimer against the other, about 15°.

22
00:01:36.047 --> 00:01:37.047
degrees.

23
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.280
The salt bridges that held T break, and the molecule moves toward R.

24
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:45.373
In T, those salt bridges resist this chain of movements.

25
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:48.453
That is why heme in T binds oxygen weakly.

26
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:52.943
Later work shows the coupling is looser than a gear train.

27
00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:57.663
Each iron shift raises the odds of the switch rather than forcing it.

28
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.187
That fits the two-shape picture.

29
00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.747
Each oxygen bound makes R more likely, not certain.

30
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.030
Perutz saw the two end points by solving deoxy and oxy hemoglobin side by side.

31
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:14.193
Do all four subunits switch together, or one at a time?

32
00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:18.013
Real hemoglobin fits neither textbook model perfectly.

33
00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.227
Its overall switch looks concerted.

34
00:02:22.087 --> 00:02:24.867
But each subunit's own shape also shifts as it binds.

35
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.310
A tiny shift at one iron, levered through a helix, raises the odds that the whole

36
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.490
molecule turns to R.
